  "prediction": "**$8.2 Billion Merger: IntercontinentalExchange to Acquire New York Stock Exchange**\n\nIn a significant move, IntercontinentalExchange Inc. (ICE) is in talks to acquire the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) in an $8.2 billion deal. The proposed merger, which is expected to be announced as early as Thursday morning, would see ICE, a commodities and derivatives trading platform, acquire the venerable NYSE.\n\nThe deal, which is valued at approximately $33 per share, represents a 37% premium to NYSE Euronext's closing price on Wednesday. ICE is expected to offer $33 per share, with two-thirds of the payment in cash and one-third in stock.\n\nThe acquisition would mark a significant shift in the global market for derivatives, which has surpassed the market for stocks. NYSE Euronext's businesses in over-the-counter trading of derivatives, including the Liffe market in London, are seen as the main attraction in the merger talks.\n\nICE, founded in 2000, has grown rapidly and now competes fiercely with the CME Group, a derivatives trading powerhouse. The company has a strong presence in commodities trading, including oil, natural gas, and agricultural products.\n\nThe proposed merger would create a massive exchange group, with 14 stock and futures exchanges and five clearing operations. The combined company would be the largest in the industry, with a significant presence in the global derivatives market.\n\nThe acquisition would also mark the end of an era for the NYSE, which has been the public face of the stock market for over two centuries. The exchange has seen a decline in trading volumes in recent years, with electronic trading platforms gaining popularity.\n\nThe deal is expected to face regulatory scrutiny, but analysts believe that it would pose fewer problems than previous mergers, as ICE focuses on commodities trading and NYSE Euronext is primarily involved in stock and options trading.\n\nIf approved, the merger would be a significant milestone in the consolidation of the exchange industry, which has seen a decade-long spree of mergers and acquisitions. The deal would also mark a triumph of electronic trading over traditional \"open outcry\" floor trading.\n\nShares of NYSE Euronext rose over 20% in after-hours trading on Wednesday, following reports of the merger talks. The deal is expected to be finalized in the coming weeks, pending regulatory approval and shareholder approval.",
